{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 fails the problem's requirement to identify what evidence within a single run's own record would distinguish genuine independent verification from mere restatement. Its counterconditions describe outside-run interventions (external tools, formally verified modules) or structural properties (prior-insensitivity), but it never specifies a countable or executable test performable on the run's own record. When the problem explicitly asks 'say exactly what to count or execute,' SRC_001 leaves that operative instruction unfulfilled.","counterexample":["A counterfactual perturbation test applied to the single-run record: locate the specific count or execution output referenced in the checking trace, substitute count±1 or a contradicting execution result, and verify whether the checking verdict flips. This is a concrete executable test on the run's own record that SRC_001 omits."],"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's perturbation-sensitivity criterion is vulnerable to a shared-prior correlation that the target itself flags but does not neutralize: its overturn_conditions admit an engine that anticipates the perturbation and games the ablation, yet it never says how to detect or exclude such gaming within a single run's record. A verdict that flips under perturbation can still be restatement if the engine's prior encodes a learned association between the claim and the specific count value; sensitivity to swapping in a contradicting value may reflect pattern completion rather than a causal dependence on an external execution result. Thus the proposed evidence is insufficient to distinguish genuine independent verification from sophisticated restatement.","counterexample":["An engine that, during training, memorized 'claim X corresponds to count 47' and 'claim not-X corresponds to count not-47' will produce a checking trace that flips its verdict when 47 is replaced by 48 — satisfying perturbation-sensitivity — while having never executed or counted anything. Its verdict is causally dependent on the substituted symbol, but only via the learned prior, not via an external fact, so SRC_002's criterion marks it as verified when it is restatement."]}]}